{
  "abstract": "We prove that a vertex-critical eight-chromatic graph of outerthickness at most two with exactly two components induced by its degree-seven vertices has a high graph of chromatic number at least six. More generally, two anticomplete connected Gallai components of total degree seven extend jointly over every five-colorable exterior in this graph class, with no bound on the order of any part. The proof coordinates recolorings of one common exterior. Independent binary splits have failure events defined by pair-parity equations, and a refinement lemma forces one component to have signed coordinates for its actual boundary columns. If the other component has a defect, fresh pair and singleton recolorings confine every defective column to the intersection of the repair pairs. Those pairs are therefore a single pair or a star. A signed-block forest argument shows that two nonadjacent rays permit an independent-triple repair. Both remaining cases force an actual seven-chromatic subgraph with independence number at most two, on at most thirteen vertices, contradicting the published outerthickness theorem for that class. Complete hand proofs credit the prior incidence framework, injective star-support and merger laws, and classical Gallai lists. Compact checks directly audit the recoloring relations, all single-pair kernel patterns and representative signed forests. The result restricts possible eight-chromatic outerthickness-two graphs, whose double-apex extensions would solve the Earth–Moon construction problem. Six-colorable high graphs, three or more low components, and the unrestricted problem remain open.",
